A||||13||Ken Wittlief||Brake vacuum assist failure - how why what?||||5||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||06-05-2001||02:21 PM||||Someone posted a while back if you have a good hard brake pedal when your engine is off, but it goes to the floor with the engine running, then your vacuum assist unit is bad and must be replaced.<P>  I get the impression they are expensive. This is what my car is doing, so I think I need one, but can anyone explain this?<P>  If I unhook and plug the vacuum hose will I at least have solid but manual brakes until I replace it? Its very disturbing when you cant lock up your brakes.<P>   is the brake pedal NOT directly linked to the master cylinder through the vacuum assist unit? is there something in there that separates pedal movement from master cylinder movement?<P>  or is it that the vacuum assist puts more pressure on the master cylinder than you can with your foot? and therefore the rest of my brake system is not right somehow?<P>   I have the factory fiero manual from the library (renewed for 3 more weeks last night) and it has drawings of it, but doesnt really explain how it works. It looks like its not something that can be repaired.<P>   so if I have to start hitting the parts car listings on ebay to get one, or the wrecking yards, Id at least like to know enough about how this works so it makes sense to me.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||06-05-2001||07:23 PM||||The test suggested is not accurate. Your problem could be either the booster or the master cyl.<P>A working booster is a required element. You should be able to get one, if you need it, from most parts stores. Running w/o it isn't an option.<P>First thing you need to check is the rest of the brakes. make sure there are no leaks or a shot MC. The system may need bleeding, but if you're way low on fluid odds are good there is a leak someplace.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||06-05-2001||09:00 PM||||If your pedal is hard with engine off and goes to the floor when engine is on, sounds like your vacuum booster is working normally and you have a problem with your master cylinder or base brake system.<P>If your fluid level is good than likely it is internal failure of your master cylinder.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||06-05-2001||10:10 PM||||Most cars' brake pedals will stiffen up after you pump the pedal a few times when the engine is off.  This is becuase as you pump them, you are using up the "vacuum assist" left.  If your pedal is immediatly stiff once the car is off, I guess I've just never encountered that in any car before.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||06-05-2001||11:00 PM||||Thanks for all the replies and suggestions. I did some tinkering and I think I know what the problem is (are).<P>  first of all I replaced the master cylinder with a rebuild monday, and the brakes are the same as they were before. so i dont think thats the problem.<P>Tonight I did take the vacuum line off the assist and plugged it, and drove the car with it off.<P> what I found was, to stop the car I had to stand on the brake pedal with both feet, and it still went all the way to the floor, and the car stopped pretty much the same as it did with the vacuum hose attached.<P>   this makes sense to me. Standing on the brakes with both feet I am applying as much pressure on the master cylinder as the vacuum assist does when I push it somewhat hard when it is powered - so its not the vacuum assist. (well unless the thing is buckeling and not holding the master cylinder in place very well).<P>   looking over the brakes again, i think I simply have crappy brakes. My rotors were rusted from sitting for two years when I got the car a month ago, and now they look glazed. <P>  so i think it need to either get all four rotors turned down, or at least take some 60 grit sandpaper to them and break the glaze off. Probabally need to do the same to the brake pads. The front ones are new - the rear ones look original.<P> I take it its not easy or cheap to replace the rotors on the fiero - I know on the saturns they are considered a maintance item, they come right off with a few taps of the hammer when the wheel is off, and they only cost about $20 each.<P>   then I guess i need to go over the calipers, wheel by wheel, and make sure they are greased like in Orges cave, and make sure all the retaining clips are holding right, and to make sure there are no air bubbles trapped in any of them.<P>  also I dont think the rear ones are adjusting up correctly. Even when I pull the hand brake up tight as i can, the parking brake will not hold the car in drive at idle. I have new ebrake cables, and a new caliper on one side - but its ebrake lever is tight and I dont think its working correctly.<P>  So I think I need to start all over again and get my sloppy brakes and calipers and rotors clean, lubed, and tighten up, and bleed them all again.<P>  I have to say, I have never had such a hard time getting the brakes to work correctly with any other car before. I guess the Fieros reputation for poor brakes is well deserved.<P>  got a little discouraged tonight when I took one of my side lights off to check the bulb, the lamp is burned out, and when I went to put it back on the metal clip it screws into broke, inside the wheel well, behind the plastic liner.<BR>   <BR>  are we having fun yet?<P>   ok i'll stop whining - I am having fun driving the car - i havent taken a car out for a spin just for the fun of it in a long time, and now I will drive my Fiero just about anywhere or any reason at the drop of a hat.<P>  and I got it down now so I can remove and stow the sunroof (or put it back on) while standing behind the opem drivers door and reaching over into the front trunk, without having to walk around the door three times.<P>  I guess it could be worse - it could be an MG (c: <P>If I wanna drive it I gotta do the annoying work too.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||06-05-2001||11:48 PM||||If the pedal goes to the floor, you probably either have air in the line, a leak somewhere, or a bad master cylinder.<P>You eliminated the MC. Is the fluid level going down after several stops? If yes you have a leak, else you probably have air in the line somewhere.<P>Still a good idea to check all the other stuff you mentioned but making sure there are no hydraulic leaks and a good job of bleeding SHOULD eliminate the pedal to the floor part of the problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||06-06-2001||12:00 AM||||I bled the brakes twice, the second time with a vacuum pump bleeder. I guess there could be air trapped in a rear caliper, like it says in the ogres cave, so I will try taking the calipers off and turning them so any air can rise to the top, and start over.<BR> <BR>  the thing that has had me going, is when the car is not running and you step on the pedal it feels firm. Its only if I step on it with both feet and push as hard as i can that it goes all the way to the floor. <BR> <BR>  I've never had to do that before to test the brakes for air - and it doesnt feel like air, it feels like something else is 'giving'.<P>   Ill get to the bottom of this eventually.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierospeeder||06-06-2001||12:10 AM||||i had my powerbrakes went out before.  <BR>I was driving and suddenly the brake pedal was almost impossible to press down.  Hard as a rock.   I pulled over and looked in the engine bay and found that the vacuum hose popped off the metal pipe that goes to the front of the car.  Put it back together and the pedal got softer to push.<P>i would turn the car on, and try pumping the brakes until the pedal is hard as a rock.  <BR>turn the car off and try bleeding again. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||06-06-2001||12:16 AM||||You're right, air should give without having to stand on it. A small leak could do it but that should show some fluid loss.<P>Seems I remember something about the back brakes causing excessive caliper piston travel if the e-brake isn't adjusted right. I don't remember the details, but that sounds like something that might give the feel you described. You mentioned you didn't think one of them was working right... sounds like a good place to start.<P>Good luck.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000009||EdsB52||06-06-2001||03:39 AM||||The brake booster is simply a large rubber diaphragm. If it goes bad, you will lose vacuum and the engine will run poorly (lean) as well as having no brakes. The reason the brakes stay good for a couple pumps after engine shut down is that the vacuum line to the booster has a check valve at the booster. Your problem is probably not the booster.||||reg||1
Z||000010||SteveJ||06-06-2001||08:33 AM||||I'm sure its not your problem but I wanted to mention that when I replaced the rotors and pads on the front the car stopped 100% better. Must have been glazed better than a town cop's doughnut.||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroJoe||06-06-2001||08:55 AM||||If they are brand new ebrake cables, they probably have to be adjusted.  The brake should hold the car after about 7 clicks.  Just retension the cables enough by tightening the cables at the equalizer.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||06-06-2001||09:46 AM||||thats another weird thing.<P>the threaded adjuster backunder the engine is screwed all the way in<P>and the ebrake lever is solid after about 6 or 7 clicks, cant pull it any tighter<P>but the ebrake still wont hold the car in drive with the engine idling at 800 rpm.<P>  but both ebrakes sides are working - if you raise the rear wheels you cant turn them by hand.<P>  something is just not right back there - the rotors are glazed or the pads arent hitting the rotor square - or the ebrake is not adjusting in like it should - <P>   I will have to start all over on the brakes on this car from square one. (Glad im not paying someone else to do all this work).<P>  that has to be a valid ebrake test - idle in drive ought to hold - i know for a mannytranny the quick buying-a-car check is to put the ebrake on hard and slowly let the clutch out with the tranny in 2nd, without giving it any gas.<P> the engine should stall and the car should not move =&gt; good ebrakes and good clutch.<P>  if the car moves the ebrakes are bad<P>  if you can let the clutch out and the car doesnt move, but the engine doesnt stall, then the clutch is bad.<P>   but for an auto Im think the ebrake should hold the car in drive, and in reverse, at idle.<P>  BTW, i did have the adjuster out looser, so the ebrake levers on the calipers would go all the way to their stops - but neither wheel seems to want to adjust in from moving the levers.<P>   maybe I should nail a 2x4 to the side of the car that scrapes against the ground when I pull back on the top end of it (c:<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Carrolles||06-06-2001||12:31 PM||||It sounds like the sleeves inside the mounting bolts on the calipers are frozen and not letting the caliper center itself on the rotor.  The result is that only one pad is contacting the rotor instead of the two pads pinching the rotor.  You would feel enough resistance so that you probably could not turn the wheel by hand with the e-brake applied but it would not hold against the engine.<P>Pull the caliper off and see if the sleeve inside the mounting bolts are free to slide from side to side.  If not, press them out with a large channel lock plier and a small socket used as a spacer.  Then spray some WD-40 in the bore and use a Q-tip or a rag wrapped around a small screw driver and swab it clean.  You can carefully pry the rubber grommet/seals out and clean them.  Clean off the sleave, lube it and put it all back together.  If you damage the grommet/seals you can get them at AutoZone for a few bucks.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
